3. Q7 — design value of P without stirrups in the shear spans
==============================================================================
level = design   branch = elasticperfectlyplastic   case = without_stirrups
  materials      f_ck =   8.71 MPa   f_cd =  5.807 MPa   f_yk =  364.5 MPa   f_yd =  317.0 MPa
  self weight    g_k  = 4.6875 kN/m   M_g = 9.223 kNm   V_g = 9.375 kN   (gamma_G = 1.35)
  bending after EC2 6.1            M_R  =   171.41 kNm  ->  P =   159.0 kN
  shear after EC2 6.2.2          V_R,c=    58.17 kN   ->  P =    45.5 kN   (rho_l = 0.00603, eq. 6.5 limit 296 kN)
  after EC2 6.2.2(6)       a_v < 2d allowance, beta = 0.8518  ->  P =    53.4 kN
  ==>  P = 45.5 kN   (shear governs)
       P_k = P / 1.5 = 30.3 kN

a/d = 1.70  ->  the point loads sit inside 2d of the supports,
so EC2 6.2.2(6) applies and is worth about 17 % on Q7. against Q6 = 159.0 kN, removing the stirrups costs 66 % ... 71 % of the design load,and the governing mode changes from bending to shear.

==============================================================================
4. Additional study — target failure probability of 1e-6, safety formats
==============================================================================
NOTE ON VINTAGE  Investigations 1-3 cite EN 1992-1-1:2004 + AC:2010, the edition in this repository. The safety-format constants below come from a DIFFERENT edition — EN 1992-1-1:2023 Annex A — and from EN 1990 and fib MC2020. EC2:2023 renumbers the standard, so 'Annex A' does not denote the same thing in the two editions and the clause numbers above do not carry over. None of those three documents is in this repository; every constant taken from them is therefore an unverified assumption here (see notes.md). The RESISTANCES being factored, by contrast, are all computed to the 2011 edition — only the factors applied to them are of the newer vintage.

Q6 and Q7 both ask for P at a target p_f = 1e-6, but name no reference period. That omission moves the answer by ~15 %, so it has to be stated:

  beta        p_f   meaning
  3.04   1.18e-03   alpha_R*beta = 0.8*3.8 — the RESISTANCE quantile behind gamma_M
  3.80   7.23e-05   RC2, 50-year reference period
  4.30   8.54e-06   RC3, 50-year
  4.70   1.30e-06   RC2,  1-year   <-- this is the match for 1e-6
  4.75   1.02e-06   exactly p_f = 1e-6

beta = 4.7 per year and beta = 3.8 per 50 years are the SAME reliability, and gamma_C = 1.5 / gamma_S = 1.15 / gamma_G = 1.35 are calibrated for it. So investigation 2 already answers Q6 — provided 1e-6 is read per year, RC2. What follows corroborates that by three independent safety formats.

level                               f_c [MPa]  f_y [MPa]   M_R [kNm]
mean            x_m                     16.71      401.0      234.02
characteristic  x_k                      8.71      364.5      203.63
GFM red. mean   x_R                      7.40      401.0      217.07
design          x_d                      8.71      364.5      171.41

ECOV   V_R      = ln(234.02/203.63)/1.645 = 0.0846
       gamma_R  = exp(0.8 * 3.8 * 0.0846) = 1.293   (GFM tabulates 1.27...1.30 — same window)

format                                         M_Rd [kNm]   P_d [kN]
PFM   EC2:2011 1.5/1.15 (investigation 2)          171.41      159.0
ECOV  R(x_m)/gamma_R/gamma_Rd                      170.73      158.3
GFM   R(x_R)/1.27          (gamma_Rd implicit)      170.92      158.5
GFM   R(x_R)/1.27/gamma_Rd (gamma_Rd explicit)      161.25      148.8

ECOV (Estimate of the Coefficient Of Variation or Cervenka's method) lands 0.4 % from the partial factor method (170.73 vs 171.41 kNm). That is the point: two formats calibrated to the same target reliability, reached from opposite ends — one from x_d, one from x_m — agree. It is the cleanest evidence that P_d from investigation 2 IS the answer at p_f = 1e-6.

GFM (Global Factor Method (also 'γ_R-method')) without gamma_Rd lands 0.3 % from the PFM (170.92 vs 171.41 kNm), which isthe arithmetic confirming the note above: dividing by 1.27 simply undoes the partial factors, because both materials were scaled up by that same 1.27. Adding gamma_Rd = 1.06 on top counts the model uncertainty twice and costs a further 6 %. Report the pair, not one of them.

NOTE 1  f_ck = f_cm - 8 implies V_fc = ln(16.71/8.71)/1.645 = 0.396,
          a 40 % CoV for concrete — absurd. The -8 MPa is an ABSOLUTE offset
          calibrated on C20-C50, where it means V ~ 0.15; on a 16.71 MPa
          concrete it is far too large. It does not spoil the V_R above only
          because this section is steel-governed in bending: a first-order
          variance propagation puts f_c at under 1 % of the variance, against
          ~34 % for f_y and ~55 % for the model uncertainty. So V_R is set by
          V_fy and V_theta alone; sweeping V_fc from 0.10 to 0.20 moves V_R by
          less than 0.001.

NOTE 2  Therefore this V_R must NOT be carried over to Q7. Eq. (6.2) goes as
          f_ck^(1/3), so there the distortion feeds straight through, and the
          model uncertainty is larger too (gamma_Rd = 1.30, not 1.06). Worse,
          C_Rd,c = 0.18/gamma_C is itself a characteristic-level constant, so
          6.2.2 cannot deliver a true R(x_m) for the ECOV numerator at all.

NOTE 3  min(P_bending, P_shear) is only valid if both modes carry their own
          gamma_Rd. With 1.06 against 1.30 the governing mode can flip between
          mean and design level (fib TG2.4.3 May 2026, 'hidden risks' slide).
          Here bending governs by 1.6x at design level,
          so it does not flip for Q6 — but that has to be shown, not assumed.
          Q7 (investigation 3) is exactly the case where it HAS flipped.
